1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,981
How does a cell know that danger is near?

2
00:00:03,031 --> 00:00:05,791
It uses pattern recognition receptors.

3
00:00:05,841 --> 00:00:09,562
For the exam, learn three things per receptor: what

4
00:00:09,612 --> 00:00:13,185
it sees, where it sits, and what signal it sends.

5
00:00:13,567 --> 00:00:19,867
Innate cells recognize molecules that whole classes of microbes share.

6
00:00:19,917 --> 00:00:25,219
These are pathogen-associated molecular patterns, or PAMPs.

7
00:00:25,269 --> 00:00:28,938
Typical PAMPs are LPS, lipoteichoic acid,

8
00:00:28,988 --> 00:00:33,656
unmethylated CpG DNA, and viral double-stranded RNA.

9
00:00:34,033 --> 00:00:40,597
Damaged or dying cells release damage-associated molecular patterns, or DAMPs.

10
00:00:40,647 --> 00:00:47,210
Examples are heat shock proteins, urate crystals, extracellular ATP and HMGB1.

11
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:52,636
DAMPs signal sterile injury, such as burns, trauma or ischaemia.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,169
The receptors for PAMPs and DAMPs are pattern recognition receptors, or PRRs.

13
00:00:58,219 --> 00:01:02,371
They sit on the cell surface, in endosomes and in the cytosol.

14
00:01:02,421 --> 00:01:06,708
Soluble recognition molecules circulate in blood and secretions.

15
00:01:07,067 --> 00:01:10,914
Think of it like smoke detectors in different rooms of a house.

16
00:01:10,964 --> 00:01:13,698
One on the outside wall sees bacterial walls.

17
00:01:13,748 --> 00:01:16,482
One in the waste room sees digested microbes.

18
00:01:16,532 --> 00:01:19,637
One in the living room sees invaders that broke in.

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,497
The best-known PRRs are the Toll-like receptors, or TLRs.

20
00:01:26,547 --> 00:01:30,058
Humans have ten, TLR1 to TLR10.

21
00:01:30,108 --> 00:01:39,706
Each TLR tail carries a TIR domain, which the IL-1, IL-18 and IL-33 receptors share.

22
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:43,897
Location splits the TLRs into two groups.

23
00:01:43,947 --> 00:01:51,657
TLR1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 sit on the plasma membrane and see bacterial and fungal PAMPs.

24
00:01:51,707 --> 00:01:57,902
TLR3, 7, 8 and 9 sit mainly in endosomes and detect nucleic acids.

25
00:01:57,952 --> 00:02:01,972
Host RNA does not normally reach endosomes,

26
00:02:02,022 --> 00:02:05,852
so location helps tell microbe from self.

27
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:08,532
Now the surface ligands.

28
00:02:08,582 --> 00:02:12,545
LPS of gram-negative bacteria binds TLR4.

29
00:02:12,595 --> 00:02:19,592
Peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid of gram-positive bacteria bind TLR2.

30
00:02:19,642 --> 00:02:24,485
TLR2 pairs with TLR1 or TLR6 to bind lipopeptides.

31
00:02:24,535 --> 00:02:28,400
Flagellin of motile bacteria binds TLR5.

32
00:02:28,767 --> 00:02:31,645
TLR4 needs helpers to see LPS.

33
00:02:31,695 --> 00:02:36,038
First, LPS binds soluble LPS-binding protein.

34
00:02:36,088 --> 00:02:40,431
CD14 is also needed for efficient signalling.

35
00:02:40,481 --> 00:02:47,753
Then the accessory protein MD2 binds lipid A, and the complex engages TLR4.

36
00:02:48,367 --> 00:02:53,533
In the endosome, TLR3 binds double-stranded RNA,

37
00:02:53,583 --> 00:02:58,859
and TLR7 and TLR8 bind viral single-stranded RNA.

38
00:02:58,909 --> 00:03:02,336
TLR9 binds unmethylated CpG DNA.

39
00:03:02,386 --> 00:03:08,205
Our own CpG is heavily methylated, so it stays silent.

40
00:03:08,255 --> 00:03:14,074
Plasmacytoid dendritic cells and B cells express TLR9.

41
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:24,088
After ligand binding, the TLR recruits adaptor proteins: MyD88, MAL, TRIF and TRAM.

42
00:03:24,138 --> 00:03:26,661
Learn three sentences.

43
00:03:26,711 --> 00:03:30,519
Every TLR except TLR3 uses MyD88.

44
00:03:30,569 --> 00:03:33,793
Only TLR3 and TLR4 use TRIF.

45
00:03:33,843 --> 00:03:38,353
So TLR4 is the only TLR that uses both.

46
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:42,015
Follow the MyD88 branch first.

47
00:03:42,065 --> 00:03:46,790
MyD88 recruits the kinases IRAK4 and IRAK1.

48
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:54,231
IRAK recruits TRAF6, which activates TAK1 and then the IKK complex.

49
00:03:54,281 --> 00:03:56,896
One part of IKK is NEMO.

50
00:03:56,946 --> 00:04:02,115
IKK phosphorylates IκB, and IκB releases NF-κB.

51
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:13,167
NF-κB switches on TNF, IL-1, IL-6, the chemokines CXCL8 and CCL2, and E-selectin.

52
00:04:13,217 --> 00:04:19,803
TLR signals also induce CD80 and CD86 on dendritic

53
00:04:19,853 --> 00:04:25,377
cells, which help start adaptive immunity.

54
00:04:25,733 --> 00:04:27,773
Now the TRIF branch.

55
00:04:27,823 --> 00:04:32,787
TRIF activates the kinases TBK1 and IKK epsilon.

56
00:04:32,837 --> 00:04:38,533
They phosphorylate IRF3, which induces interferon beta.

57
00:04:38,583 --> 00:04:41,354
TLR3 uses only this branch.

58
00:04:41,404 --> 00:04:48,771
TLR4 uses both, so LPS gives an inflammatory and an antiviral response.

59
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:55,624
The endosomal TLR7, 8 and 9 use MyD88 too, but with a twist.

60
00:04:55,674 --> 00:04:59,984
In endosomes, MyD88 binds IRF7 directly.

61
00:05:00,034 --> 00:05:04,781
So TLR7 and TLR9 make plasmacytoid dendritic

62
00:05:04,831 --> 00:05:09,141
cells produce interferon alpha and beta.

63
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:12,702
What happens when the MyD88 branch fails?

64
00:05:12,752 --> 00:05:17,143
This pathway sits under most TLRs and the IL-1 receptor.

65
00:05:17,193 --> 00:05:21,108
Children with MyD88 or IRAK4 deficiency get severe

66
00:05:21,158 --> 00:05:25,470
invasive bacterial infections, especially pneumococcal.

67
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:31,497
They often have no fever and little inflammation: so-called cold infections.

68
00:05:31,867 --> 00:05:37,778
Infections improve with age, and IRAK4 deficiency adds no viral risk.

69
00:05:37,828 --> 00:05:40,025
Now break the TLR3 branch.

70
00:05:40,075 --> 00:05:44,777
TLR3 loss causes recurrent herpes simplex encephalitis,

71
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:49,011
but other viral infections do not generally rise.

72
00:05:49,367 --> 00:05:55,290
Defects in TRIF, TRAF3, TBK1 or UNC93B do the same.

73
00:05:55,340 --> 00:06:02,552
UNC93B moves TLRs from the endoplasmic reticulum to endosomes.

74
00:06:02,933 --> 00:06:04,456
Next, the cytosol.

75
00:06:04,506 --> 00:06:10,745
NOD1 and NOD2 are NOD-like receptors that sense bacterial peptidoglycan.

76
00:06:10,795 --> 00:06:16,510
NOD1 sees diaminopimelic acid, mainly from gram-negative bacteria.

77
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:22,974
NOD2 sees muramyl dipeptide from gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria.

78
00:06:23,024 --> 00:06:27,516
Both recruit the kinase RIP2, which activates NF-κB.

79
00:06:27,867 --> 00:06:32,460
NOD2 is a classic exam trap, because both directions cause disease.

80
00:06:32,510 --> 00:06:38,213
Some NOD2 variants sense gut microbes poorly and raise the risk of Crohn's disease.

81
00:06:38,263 --> 00:06:41,817
Gain-of-function NOD2 mutations cause Blau syndrome,

82
00:06:41,867 --> 00:06:44,797
with inflammation of joints, eyes and skin.

83
00:06:45,367 --> 00:06:49,591
Now the inflammasome, a multiprotein complex that

84
00:06:49,641 --> 00:06:53,691
forms in the cytosol after infection or injury.

85
00:06:53,741 --> 00:06:55,785
IL-1β needs two signals.

86
00:06:55,835 --> 00:07:03,026
Signal 1 comes through NF-κB, for example from a TLR, and makes inactive pro-IL-1β.

87
00:07:03,076 --> 00:07:08,435
Signal 2 activates the inflammasome, which cuts the precursor.

88
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:15,613
Common triggers of signal 2 are potassium efflux and reactive oxygen species.

89
00:07:15,663 --> 00:07:19,088
Crystals may act by damaging lysosomes.

90
00:07:19,138 --> 00:07:23,901
NLRP3 molecules then cluster and bind the adaptor ASC.

91
00:07:23,951 --> 00:07:29,070
ASC recruits procaspase-1, which becomes active caspase-1.

92
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:34,507
Caspase-1 cuts pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into active cytokines.

93
00:07:34,867 --> 00:07:38,002
Caspase-1 also cleaves gasdermin D, and its

94
00:07:38,052 --> 00:07:41,262
fragment forms pores in the plasma membrane.

95
00:07:41,312 --> 00:07:46,522
IL-1β has no signal peptide, so it leaves the cell through these pores.

96
00:07:46,572 --> 00:07:50,744
The pores also let fluid in, so the cell swells and dies.

97
00:07:50,794 --> 00:07:53,559
This inflammatory death is pyroptosis.

98
00:07:53,609 --> 00:07:58,301
It affects macrophages and dendritic cells, but not neutrophils.

99
00:07:58,667 --> 00:08:03,378
NLRP3 senses crystals, silica, extracellular

100
00:08:03,428 --> 00:08:08,464
ATP, some bacterial toxins and influenza virus.

101
00:08:08,514 --> 00:08:15,498
It also senses alum, and alum's adjuvant effect depends on NLRP3.

102
00:08:15,867 --> 00:08:20,692
Gain-of-function mutations in inflammasome parts cause too much IL-1.

103
00:08:20,742 --> 00:08:25,992
Patients get recurrent fever with inflammation of skin, joints and abdomen.

104
00:08:26,042 --> 00:08:28,535
These are autoinflammatory diseases.

105
00:08:28,585 --> 00:08:34,259
Unlike autoimmune diseases, they do not need self-reactive antibodies or T cells.

106
00:08:34,633 --> 00:08:39,096
The key example is CAPS, the cryopyrin-associated periodic

107
00:08:39,146 --> 00:08:42,909
syndromes, from gain-of-function NLRP3 mutations.

108
00:08:42,959 --> 00:08:48,200
In familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome, cold triggers the fever.

109
00:08:48,250 --> 00:08:51,934
Muckle-Wells syndrome adds progressive deafness.

110
00:08:51,984 --> 00:08:56,369
Both give urticarial rash, joint pain and conjunctivitis.

111
00:08:56,733 --> 00:09:01,999
In the textbooks, IL-1 antagonists such as anakinra treat CAPS successfully.

112
00:09:02,049 --> 00:09:05,565
Drug choices change, so check the current guidance.

113
00:09:05,933 --> 00:09:13,288
Viral RNA in the cytosol meets the RIG-like receptors, RIG-I and MDA5.

114
00:09:13,338 --> 00:09:17,943
RIG-I recognizes RNA with a 5′-triphosphate.

115
00:09:17,993 --> 00:09:23,020
Host mRNA has a cap instead, so it stays silent.

116
00:09:23,070 --> 00:09:27,357
MDA5 recognizes long double-stranded RNA.

117
00:09:27,733 --> 00:09:34,867
Activated RIG-I or MDA5 binds MAVS on the outer mitochondrial membrane.

118
00:09:34,917 --> 00:09:40,635
MAVS recruits TRAF proteins, which activate TBK1 and IKK.

119
00:09:40,685 --> 00:09:45,795
Then IRF3, IRF7 and NF-κB induce type I interferon.

120
00:09:46,167 --> 00:09:50,536
Cytosolic double-stranded DNA is a danger sign too.

121
00:09:50,586 --> 00:09:55,908
It activates the enzyme cGAS, which makes the messenger cGAMP.

122
00:09:55,958 --> 00:09:59,981
cGAMP binds STING on the endoplasmic reticulum.

123
00:10:00,031 --> 00:10:06,047
STING activates TBK1, so both routes meet: IRF3 and type I interferon.

124
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:09,324
Too much interferon also causes disease.

125
00:10:09,374 --> 00:10:13,339
Gain-of-function STING mutations cause SAVI, with skin

126
00:10:13,389 --> 00:10:17,056
vasculopathy, fever and interstitial lung disease.

127
00:10:17,106 --> 00:10:20,922
In Aicardi-Goutières syndrome, faulty nucleases such

128
00:10:20,972 --> 00:10:24,714
as TREX1 let excess nucleic acid reach the sensors.

129
00:10:25,067 --> 00:10:27,602
Why does this matter in allergy?

130
00:10:27,652 --> 00:10:29,542
Mast cells express TLRs.

131
00:10:29,592 --> 00:10:33,986
One Abbas chapter says TLR ligands cause degranulation;

132
00:10:34,036 --> 00:10:38,188
another says mediator release without degranulation.

133
00:10:38,238 --> 00:10:42,390
IL-33 sits in the nuclei of barrier epithelial cells

134
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:46,673
and acts as an alarmin when damaged cells release it.

135
00:10:47,033 --> 00:10:49,505
Five things to remember.

136
00:10:49,555 --> 00:10:57,071
One: surface TLRs see microbial walls; endosomal TLRs see nucleic acids.

137
00:10:57,121 --> 00:11:00,855
Two: all TLRs except TLR3 use MyD88.

138
00:11:00,905 --> 00:11:04,112
Three: IL-1β needs two signals.

139
00:11:04,162 --> 00:11:09,997
Four: RNA and DNA sensors converge on type I interferon.

140
00:11:10,047 --> 00:11:15,251
Five: MyD88 or IRAK4 defects give cold infections;

141
00:11:15,301 --> 00:11:19,244
TLR3 defects give herpes encephalitis.

142
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:20,601
One exam trap.

143
00:11:20,651 --> 00:11:26,382
TLR4 sits on the cell surface, yet it still uses TRIF and induces interferon.

144
00:11:26,432 --> 00:11:29,760
Next: phagocytosis and the respiratory burst,

145
00:11:29,810 --> 00:11:32,913
what the cell does after it senses danger.
